---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import WorkIllustration from "../../components/WorkIllustration.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="HefaOS: an open stack for building real robots"
  description="Why I am building HefaOS, an open-source robotics framework pairing a declarative TypeScript SDK with a real-time C++ runtime, hardware abstraction, simulation, and explicit execution constraints."
>
  <main class="project-page hefaos-page">
    <section class="project-hero">
      <div class="shell">
        <a class="back-link" href="/#work">← Back to selected work</a>
        <div class="project-hero-grid">
          <div class="project-hero-copy">
            <p class="eyebrow">Selected work / HefaOS</p>
            <h1>Build the robot. Not its infrastructure. Again.</h1>
            <p class="project-lede">
              HefaOS is an open-source attempt to make the foundations of a robot reusable: describe hardware, tasks, and behaviour in TypeScript, then run them through a C++ system designed for the less negotiable parts of the physical world.
            </p>
            <div class="project-actions">
              <a class="oval-link" href="https://github.com/alokdidde/HefaOS" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Browse HefaOS ↗</a>
              <a class="arrow-link" href="https://github.com/alokdidde/HefaOS/blob/master/hefaos-os-specification.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the architecture <span>↗</span></a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="project-hero-visual" aria-label="Animated diagram of the HefaOS robotics system">
            <WorkIllustration id="hefaos-page" visual="robotics" />
            <div class="project-visual-caption">
              <span>DEFINE / COMPILE / RUN</span>
              <strong>Readable above.<br />Predictable below.</strong>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <dl class="project-facts">
          <div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Open-source framework</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Authoring</dt><dd>TypeScript + TSX</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Runtime</dt><dd>Modern C++</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Early architecture</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-statement">
      <div class="shell project-statement-grid">
        <p class="section-index">01 / Why it exists</p>
        <div>
          <h2>Robotics has a habit of making every new machine begin with plumbing.</h2>
          <div class="project-copy-columns">
            <p>
              A robot project quickly accumulates drivers, state, scheduling, message transport, configuration, deployment, simulation, model runtimes, and safety boundaries. Much of it is necessary. Rather less of it is the reason anybody wanted to build the robot.
            </p>
            <p>
              Existing frameworks solve a great deal, but the path from a readable robot definition to deterministic execution still asks teams to hold several mental models at once. HefaOS asks whether that boundary can become narrower, more explicit, and pleasantly boring.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-system-section">
      <div class="shell">
        <header class="project-section-heading">
          <p class="section-index">02 / The model</p>
          <h2>Friendly at design time. Strict at run time.</h2>
          <p>
            The architecture separates how a robot is described from how it executes. TypeScript provides the vocabulary. The compiler checks and lowers it. C++ deals with timing, hardware, and the consequences.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="project-principles">
          <article>
            <span>01</span>
            <h3>Robots are declared as systems</h3>
            <p>Components, sensors, actuators, tasks, behaviours, rates, deadlines, and data dependencies live together in a readable TypeScript definition.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>02</span>
            <h3>The compiler makes constraints concrete</h3>
            <p>The intended compiler validates the graph and emits the configuration and bindings required by the runtime. Invalid timing should fail before a motor develops an opinion.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>03</span>
            <h3>The runtime owns execution</h3>
            <p>A C++ core is designed around explicit task graphs, bounded state, hardware access, and deterministic scheduling rather than a cheerful collection of background promises.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>04</span>
            <h3>Hardware and simulation share a boundary</h3>
            <p>A common abstraction layer is intended to let the same robot definition meet Linux hardware, test doubles, or simulation without pretending those environments are identical.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-workflow-section">
      <div class="shell project-workflow-grid">
        <div class="project-workflow-intro">
          <p class="section-index">03 / The intended loop</p>
          <h2>Describe once. Challenge often.</h2>
          <p>The specification is trying to make the path from an idea to a tested physical system easier to inspect.</p>
        </div>

        <ol class="project-workflow-list">
          <li>
            <span>01</span>
            <div><h3>Define the machine</h3><p>Describe joints, sensors, actuators, limits, frames, and target hardware as named components rather than scattering them across configuration files and constructor folklore.</p></div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span>02</span>
            <div><h3>State the work and its timing</h3><p>Tasks declare periods, deadlines, priority, inputs, and outputs. The execution contract becomes part of the source rather than a fact discovered during an especially educational oscillation.</p></div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span>03</span>
            <div><h3>Compile and inspect the graph</h3><p>Validate dependencies and target capabilities, then produce artefacts the runtime can execute and a human can examine.</p></div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span>04</span>
            <div><h3>Move from simulation to hardware</h3><p>Exercise the same system against simulated and physical backends, keeping the differences visible instead of hiding them beneath a particularly optimistic adapter.</p></div>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-origin-section">
      <div class="shell project-origin-grid">
        <p class="section-index">04 / Why I am building it</p>
        <div>
          <h2>The abstraction should help without lying.</h2>
          <p class="project-origin-lede">
            I wanted a robotics stack that is approachable from software, but remains honest about time, hardware, and failure.
          </p>
          <div class="project-copy-columns">
            <p>
              High-level tools are useful because they let more people express a system clearly. They become dangerous when ease of expression is mistaken for ease of execution. A one-millisecond task is not made punctual by placing it inside a nicer API.
            </p>
            <p>
              HefaOS therefore keeps the authoring model and runtime model distinct but connected. The ambition is not to disguise the machinery. It is to give each concern a sensible home, then make the boundaries inspectable enough that somebody else can build on them.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-truth-section">
      <div class="shell project-truth-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="section-index">05 / Current truth</p>
          <h2>The specification is ahead of the implementation.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="project-truth-copy">
          <p>
            HefaOS is public at an early stage. The operating-system, SDK, and development-infrastructure specifications are substantial, and the repository contains the intended package structure, build system, CI, development environment, and a declarative robotic-arm example.
          </p>
          <p>
            Large parts of the runtime, hardware layer, AI backends, compiler, and tests are still scaffolding. There are no production timing results to publish yet. The repository is an architectural commitment and a place to do the work, not evidence that the work has politely completed itself.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Public and open source under the MIT licence</li>
            <li>C++ runtime and TypeScript SDK architecture</li>
            <li>Hardware, simulation, and AI boundaries specified</li>
            <li>Implementation and benchmarks still in progress</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-cta-section">
      <div class="shell project-cta-grid">
        <p class="section-index">Explore the project</p>
        <h2>A foundation for robots, still being poured.</h2>
        <div class="project-cta-actions">
          <a class="oval-link oval-link-large" href="https://github.com/alokdidde/HefaOS" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Browse HefaOS ↗</a>
          <a class="arrow-link" href="https://github.com/alokdidde/HefaOS/blob/master/hefaos-sdk-specification.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the SDK specification <span>↗</span></a>
          <a class="arrow-link" href="https://github.com/alokdidde/HefaOS/blob/master/examples/simple-arm/robot.tsx" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">See the robot definition <span>↗</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
